  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 112 AM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nStrong 1042 mb surface high pressure is currently centered over\neastern MT and northern WY. A 1006 mb surface low is also \ncentered over the Great Lakes. Between these features, an\nelevated pressure gradient is present, leading to breezy\nnorthwest winds across the forecast area later this morning. \nBumped up the winds and gusts a few notches over NBM consensus, \nso expect winds 20-30 mph with gusts 30-40 mph. Winds will\ngradually diminish as aforementioned high pressure begins to \nmove southward through this afternoon and situates over the \narea late this afternoon. With clear skies and calm winds, \nexpect a widespread freeze tonight and into Saturday morning. A\nFreeze Watch has been issued for the entire forecast area to \naccount for this likelihood. Areas west of Route 283 will have a\nbetter potential for a harder, killing freeze with temperatures\ndropping into the middle 20s. Areas along and east of Route 283\nstill has a high probability of freezing temperatures, but \ngenerally temperatures are expected to be in the 29-32 degree \nrange. These colder temperatures are short lived as return \nsoutherly flow emerges as the high pressure continues eastward \nover the central Mississippi Valley into this weekend. High \ntemperatures are expected to be in the 60s on Saturday, with 70s\nreturning Sunday. Lows in this timeframe will be in the mid to \nupper 30s Sunday morning, and 40s by Monday morning.\n\nTemperatures continue to gradually build back around 80 by mid\nto late week as a ridge builds in ahead of a deepening and \ndigging trough across the Western U.S. through next week. Our \nnext chance of precipitation increases by late week next week \nand perhaps into next weekend. Globals differ on timing and \nplacement of a potential development of a possible lee cyclone.\nGFS is a bit more progressive and farther north with the ENS \nslower, stronger, and farther south. In either case, should be \non the warm side of the storm. Instability is meager among the \nmodels, leading to thunderstorm chance uncertainty. With the \nsweeping cold front getting well into the Gulf, any potential \nmoisture return back into our area may be difficult to \naccomplish. POPs are low in the forecast as of now, with only \nchance categories around 30-40% Thursday and Friday. \nClimatologically, continued widespread rains like we've seen \nare harder to come by in these typically drier months. \nOtherwise, plenty of time to see how this upcoming pattern \nshakes out.",
